  "generated_text": "To convert $\\sqrt{2} e^{11 \\pi i/4}$ to rectangular form, we use Euler's formula:\n\\[ e^{11 \\pi i/4} = \\cos(11 \\pi/4) + i \\sin(11 \\pi/4) \\]\n\\[ \\cos(11 \\pi/4) = \\cos(3 \\pi/4) = -\\sqrt{2}/2 \\]\n\\[ \\sin(11 \\pi/4) = \\sin(3 \\pi/4) = -\\sqrt{2}/2 \\]\nThus,\n\\[ e^{11 \\pi i/4} = -\\sqrt{2}/2 - i \\sqrt{2}/2 \\]\nMultiplying by $\\sqrt{2}$:\n\\[ \\sqrt{2} e^{11 \\pi i/4} = -\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}/2 - i \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}/2 \\]\n\\[ = -1 - i \\]\nSo, the rectangular form of $\\sqrt{2} e^{11 \\pi i/4}$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1 - i}\n\\]",
